Specification Functional Specification Document - Taschenrechner - Grundrechenarten-Modul1. Overview
This module is designed to implement the fundamental arithmetic operations (Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, Division). Its purpose is to create a reusable library for internal projects that require basic arithmetic functionalities.

2. User Stories
As a developer, I want to be able to easily integrate the Grundrechenarten-Modul into my project, so that I can save time and effort in implementing basic arithmetic operations.
As an end-user, I want the module to accurately perform addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, so that I can rely on the results for my calculations.
As an end-user, I want the module to prevent division by zero and provide detailed error messages when encountering non-numeric values, NaN, or Infinity.
As an end-user, I want the module to support decimal values, handle large numbers as needed, and consider both positive and negative numbers.

3. Functional Requirements
SHALL correctly perform addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division operations.
SHOULD provide an error message when division by zero occurs, detailing the issue and suggesting the appropriate action to resolve it.
MAY support other advanced mathematical functions as future requirements, such as square root, logarithm, trigonometric functions, or exponentiation.

4. User Workflows
Primary use case: Performing arithmetic operations
The user calls the appropriate arithmetic function (e.g., add, subtract, multiply, or divide) of the Grundrechenarten-Modul.
The module performs the specified arithmetic operation using the provided input values, considering their validity and range.
The module returns the result of the operation, corresponding to the specified arithmetic function. In case of an error, the module should provide a descriptive error message.

5. UI/UX Considerations
The module should have a user-friendly API for easy integration, allowing developers to quickly access and implement the necessary functions.
The error message for division by zero should be clear and concise, describing the reason for the error and suggesting a remedy for the user to take corrective actions.

6. Data Requirements
Input data: Two numbers for addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division operations. Output data: The result of the performed arithmetic operation.
The module should support decimal values and handle large numbers as needed.
The module should consider both positive and negative numbers, ensuring proper sign handling for accurate results.

7. Integration Points
The Grundrechenarten-Modul is intended to be integrated into various internal projects. No external APIs or services are required at this time.

8. Constraints and Assumptions
The module assumes that the input data is always a valid numerical value (integer or decimal).
The module does not handle complex numbers or scientific calculations.

9. Open Questions
Should the module provide unit testing examples for developers to easily test the integration? To ensure the correct functionality of the Grundrechenarten-Modul during its integration, it is recommended that unit testing examples be provided.

This enhanced functional specification document provides a more comprehensive understanding of the Grundrechenarten-Modul's purpose, requirements, and intended usage, addressing the selected improvements for a more robust and user-friendly solution.</pre></body></html>